  "name": "The Distribution, Abundance, and Diversity of the Epifauna of Two Bays (Alitak and Ugak) of Kodiak Island, Alaska",
  "description": "This report references population abundance, production, and species composition data of invertebrate communities; diet data of fish species; Ammodytes spp. and Clupea pallasi; and diet and species composition data of invertebrate species: Clinocardium ciliatum, Clinocardium nuttali, Evasterias troschelii, Macoma calcarea, Macoma moesta, Macoma spp., Modiolus modiolus, Mytilus trossulus, Protothaca staminea, Pycnopodia helianthoides, Saxidomus giganteus, Siliqua alta, Spisula polynyma, Strongylocentrotus droebachiensis, and Telmessus cheiragonus, from Alitak Bay and Ugak Bay, Kodiak Island, Alaska. The aforementioned data are referenced in citation: Feder, H. M. and S. C. Jewett. 1977. The distribution, abundance, and diversity of the epifauna of two bays (Alitak and Ugak) of Kodiak Island, Alaska. In Environmental Assessment of the Alaskan Continental Shelf: Annual reports of principal investigators for the year ending March 1977.Vol. 10. Receptors-Fish, Littoral, Benthos. RU-29. pp. 527-586. This literature was collected as part of a metadatabase that represents one or more nearshore resources (from physical attributes such as water and air, to birds and mammals). Each entry is viewable in three formats, 1) a geospatial explicit format (ArcView 3.3) named GIS ArcView 3.3 Near GEM project, 2) a spreadsheet format (Excel 2002 and within ArcView) named GEM Nearshore Metadata Set, and 3) a ProCite database named GEM Nearshore Metadata References, which consists of references included in 1 and 2 above. The ProCite database record number corresponds to the Excel Procite Number. Reference Procite #315 for this literature's metadata. The metadata are maintained in digital format (ArcView 3.3, Excel 2002, and Procite) and are available from the Alaska Science Center, USGS in Anchorage, Alaska. For a detailed description of the three databases and the project, see \"Bodkin, J. L., and T. Dean. 2003. Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project G-030687), US Geological Survey, Alaska Science Center, Anchorage, Alaska.\" available on-line at http://www.evostc.state.ak.us/pdf/GEM_final_reports/030687.pdf.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: Part of the design effort of the \"Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring\" (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project). Provides a comprehensive historical perspective of locations and types of past studies conducted in the nearshore marine communities within Gulf of Alaska in the form of a geographical information system database.",
